.TH DRAWBIND 1
.SH NAME
drawbind \- what a program's own bind does to a trace of it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B drawbind
[
.B -b
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Drawbind
allocates a display and a 64\(mu64 image through
.IR draw (2)
and says so.
With
.BR -b ,
it binds
.B "#i"
onto
.B /dev
first; without it, it uses whatever
.B /dev
it inherited.
Nothing else differs.
.PP
It exists because that difference decides whether the work can be
recorded.
.IR Iostats (4)
interposes on the
.I name space,
so it sees what a program reaches through the name space \(em and
.B bind
of a kernel device goes straight to the device, past anything mounted on
the way.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
% bind -b '#i' /dev
% iostats -t /a.styx drawbind
drawbind: allocated a 64x64 image, using an inherited /dev
% iostats -t /b.styx drawbind -b
drawbind: allocated a 64x64 image, having bound #i onto /dev
% styxlog -f draw /a.styx
    0.023  open   /dev/draw/new
    0.023  open   /dev/draw/1/data
    0.023  open   /dev/draw/1/refresh
    0.024  write  /dev/draw/1/data (51 bytes)
    ...
% styxlog -f draw /b.styx
%
.EE
.PP
Both runs allocated the image.
Only one of them can be seen to have done so.
.PP
Running the same program both ways is the part that matters.
An empty trace is not evidence on its own \(em a program that did nothing
produces one too \(em so the case worth showing is the one where the work
demonstrably happened and the trace is still empty.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/drawbind.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR iostats (4),
.IR styxlog (1),
.IR styxreplay (1),
.IR draw (2)
.SH BUGS
It demonstrates the gap and does not close it.
Closing it would mean interposing below the name space rather than in
it.
.PP
.B "#i"
is the case that matters in practice, but nothing about it is special:
any
.B "bind #"
escapes a trace the same way.
